'''Anthrenus''' (an-thrē′nus), ''n.'' [NL., < Gr. ἀνθρήνη, a hornet, wasp; cf. ἀνθρηδών, a hornet, τενθρήνη, τενθρηδών, a hornet, orig. any buzzing insect; cf. ''drone''. See ''Andrenidæ''.] A notable genus of beetles,

readings.c3s2:
of the family ''Dermestidæ'', certain species of which are well known as museum pests. {{smaller|Such are ''A. varius'' (Fabricius) and ''A. musæorum'', small gray species spotted with brown, which do great injury to collections of natural history. ''A. scrophulariæ'', a larger species, black, red, and white, is known as the carpet-beetle and buffalo-bug, and is very destructive to carpets and other woolen fabrics. See cut under ''carpet-beetle''.}}

'''anthribid''' (an′thri-bid), ''n.'' A beetle of the family ''Anthribidæ''.

'''Anthribidæ''' (an-thrib′i-dē), ''n. pl.'' [NL., < ''Anthribus'' + ''-idæ''.] A family of rhynchophorous ''Coleoptera'', typified by the genus ''Anthribus''. {{smaller|These snout-beetles have a strong fold on the inner face of each elytron, the pygidium in both sexes undivided and normal, the last spiracle uncovered, the tibiæ not serrate, and the straight antennæ with 10 or 11 joints.}}

'''Anthribus''' (an′thri-bus), ''n.'' [NL. (Geoffroy, 1764), also ''Anthribidus'' and ''Anthotribidus'', appar. < Gr. ἄνθος, a flower; the second element is not clear.] A genus of rhynchophorous beetles, giving name to the family ''Anthribidæ''.

'''anthropic''' (an-throp′ik), ''a.'' [< Gr. ἀνθρωπικός, of man, human, < ἄνθρωπος, a man, a human being; perhaps for *ἄνδρωπος, lit. having a human face or appearance, < ἀνήρ (ἀνδρ-), a man, + ὤψ (ὠπ-), face, countenance, eye: see ''andro-'' and ''optic''.] Belonging to man; manlike; sprung from man; human.
{{smaller block|If we leave the region of formulas and go back to the practical effect of religion on human conduct, we must be driven to the conclusion that the future of religion is to be, not only what every real religion has ever been, anthropomorphic, but frankly ''anthropic''.}}
